{"about":"7043957a83082eac14fe82fd2fee22d8e8267cf00f35e5ac504f3b9944aae437","body":"The preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible on Popper's own terms if understood as a pragmatic, non-inductive rule of action. Popper distinguishes between the logical status of a theory (which remains unproven and improbable) and the rational decision of which theory to rely upon for critical discussion or practical application. Choosing the best-tested theory does not imply an inductive belief that it will remain true; rather, it acknowledges that this theory has withstood the most severe attempts at falsification so far. This approach avoids smuggling in induction because it makes no claim about future success probabilities, only that the selected theory is currently the most worthy of scrutiny and tentative reliance. The cost of accepting this view is the abandonment of any hope for positive justification or certainty; we act on the best available option while fully accepting that it may be falsified tomorrow, retaining a stance of permanent fallibilism.","kind":"mini.commitment-proposal.v1","named":"The Defensibility of Corroboration as Pragmatic Choice"}